---
/**
 * Home page.
 *
 * A landing rather than a feed: a preview of what the site holds, with a route
 * into each section. The full catalog moved to /palettes/, which is what the
 * Explore button points at.
 *
 * The palette grid is worth a note. Popular is ranked live from Firebase, but
 * the page ships with 20 statically rendered palettes so it is never blank
 * before that resolves, and so the HTML contains real content for crawlers,
 * which never see the Firebase result. gallery.js swaps them for the live
 * ranking once it arrives.
 */
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Feed from '../components/Feed.astro';
import { getFirstRows, getMeta } from '../lib/data.server.js';
import { FEATURED_COLORS } from '../lib/colors.js';
import { isDark } from '../lib/palette.js';

const meta = getMeta();
const preRows = getFirstRows('new', 20);

const tagCount = meta.tags.length;

const site = Astro.site ?? new URL('https://palette-vault.example');
const schema = [
	{
		'@type': 'CollectionPage',
		name: 'Color palettes',
		description: `Browse four-color palettes and ${FEATURED_COLORS.length}+ colors, with hex codes for each.`,
		url: `${site.origin}/`,
	},
];
---

<Layout
	title='Color Palettes, Free Palettes and Hex Codes'
	description={'Browse color palettes built in OKLCH, plus hex codes and shades for ' +
		'every color. Click a swatch to copy it. Free for commercial use, no sign-up.'}
	active='new'
	schema={schema}>
	<Feed
		title='Color palettes for designers and developers'
		subtitle='Four-color palettes built in OKLCH. Click any swatch to copy its HEX.'
		type='popular'
		limit={20}
		preRows={preRows}
		infinite={false}
		emptyTitle='No palettes yet'
		emptyText='Run npm run generate to build the dataset.'>
		<div
			slot='intro'
			class='page-intro'>
			<p>
				Every color palette here is four colors chosen to work together, ready to drop into a
				design, a website or a slide deck. Click a swatch to copy its HEX code, or open a palette to
				take all four at once and download it as a PNG. Free for commercial work, no attribution, no
				sign-up.
			</p>
		</div>

		<div class='section-cta'>
			<a class="btn btn--primary" href="/palettes/">Explore palettes</a>
			<span class='section-cta__note'>The full catalog, loading as you scroll</span>
		</div>

		<!-- ------------------------------------------------------------ colors -->
		<section class='home-section'>
			<header class='home-section__head'>
				<h2>Colors</h2>
				<p> Hex codes, shade scales and matching palettes for every color name. </p>
			</header>

			<ul class='color-row'>
				{
					FEATURED_COLORS.map((color) => (
						<li>
							<a
								class='color-tile'
								href={`/colors/${color.slug}/`}>
								<span
									class:list={['color-tile__swatch', isDark(color.hex) ? 'on-dark' : 'on-light']}
									style={`background:#${color.hex}`}>
									<span class='color-tile__hex'>#{color.hex.toUpperCase()}</span>
								</span>
								<span class='color-tile__name'>{color.name}</span>
							</a>
						</li>
					))
				}
			</ul>

			<div class='section-cta'>
				<a class="btn" href="/colors/">View all colors</a>
			</div>
		</section>

		<!-- ------------------------------------------------------------- prose -->
		<section class='page-note'>
			<h2>What makes a four-color palette work</h2>

			<p>
				Every palette here is built in <strong>OKLCH</strong>, a perceptually uniform color space,
				and that choice does most of the work. In OKLCH an equal step in lightness looks like an
				equal step to the eye, which is not true of RGB or HSL, in HSL a yellow and a blue at the
				same stated lightness look nothing alike. Working in a space that matches perception is why
				these four-color sets hold together instead of reading as four colors that happen to share
				a card.
			</p>

			<p>
				Beyond the color space, three things separate a palette that works from one that merely
				varies. The four hues follow a classical relationship, monochrome, analogous,
				complementary, split complementary, triad or tetrad, rather than sitting at arbitrary
				angles. Lightness runs as a deliberate ladder from light to dark, because a palette with
				four mid-tones has nowhere to put text. And chroma tapers at the extremes, since very light
				and very dark colors physically hold less of it, which is what keeps the pale end from
				going chalky and the dark end from turning to mud.
			</p>

			<h2>Finding the color palette you need</h2>

			<p>
				The sidebar holds {tagCount} categories. Style tags, pastel, vintage, retro, neon, warm, cold,
				sunset, night, describe the mood, while color tags narrow things to a family such as blue, green
				or pink. Tags come from the measured result rather than the intent behind it: a palette appears
				under <a href="/tag/blue/" title="Blue color palettes">Blue</a>{' '}
				because its colors measure as blue, not because the preset that produced it was aiming that way.
			</p>

			<p>
				If you already have one color in mind, the{' '}
				<a href="/tools/generate/" title="Generate color palettes from a base color">palette generator</a> builds a scheme around it and lets you lock the shades worth keeping. To pull a palette out
				of a photograph instead, the{' '}
				<a href="/tools/extract/" title="Extract a color palette from an image">image extractor</a> clusters the pixels and returns the colors that actually define the picture. There are eight{' '}
				<a href="/tools/" title="Free color tools">free color tools</a>{' '}
				in all, and every one of them runs entirely in your browser.
			</p>
		</section>
	</Feed>
</Layout>
